# ── Unpublished working docs ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The full documentation set is kept locally as the working copy. Only the
# README is published, so a reader gets the landing page without the
# internal runbooks. Delete this block to publish docs/ as well.
docs/

# ── Terraform ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Provider binaries and state stay out: .terraform/ is hundreds of MB and
# *.tfstate holds plaintext resource attributes.
#
# The root module's lock file IS committed (see the negation below). It is
# ~100 lines of provider versions and checksums, and Terraform expects it in
# version control. Without it `init` silently resolves to whatever provider
# version is newest, so a plan that passed on Monday can fail on Tuesday with
# no code change — and the h1: hashes are how a tampered provider is caught.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Child modules are never applied directly, so their lock files are just
# leftovers from a standalone `terraform init` and Terraform ignores them
# during a root-module apply.
.terraform.lock.hcl
!infra/envs/dev/.terraform.lock.hcl

crash.log
crash.*.log
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Terraform state holds plaintext credentials. Never commit it.
*.pem
*.key
!*.pub
.env
.env.*
*.csv
credentials
.aws/

